  "id": "COMPOUND:980 - PROTEIN:5253",
  "verbalization": "Doxorubicin is implicated in the interaction with TE-12 through multiple distinct pathways, each involving different molecular targets. The first pathway links doxorubicin to Probable G-protein coupled receptor 132, which is targeted by Racotumomab, ultimately leading to TE-12. The second pathway connects doxorubicin to P2X purinoceptor 1, which is targeted by Mollicellin N, also leading to TE-12. The third pathway involves doxorubicin targeting COLO-792, which is then targeted by a compound named 3Beta-Hydroxyolean-12-En-23,29-Dioic Acid, which also leads to TE-12. These diverse targets suggest that doxorubicin may exert its effects through multiple mechanisms, potentially involving different cellular pathways or signaling cascades. The integration of these pathways highlights the complexity of doxorubicin's interaction with TE-12, emphasizing the importance of understanding the underlying molecular mechanisms that connect these entities.",
